---
import Layout from "../../layouts/website-builder.astro";

const fontExample = `@font-face {
  font-family: "Example Sans";
  src: url("/fonts/example-sans.woff2") format("woff2");
  font-style: normal;
  font-weight: 400;
  font-display: swap;
}`;
---

<Layout
  title="Add custom fonts"
  description="A guide to self-hosting fonts in your website project."
>
  <article class="mx-auto grid max-w-3xl gap-5 p-6 text-ink-page sm:p-8">
    <h2 class="font-display text-2xl font-bold">Add custom fonts</h2>
    <p>
      Self-host a licensed font so the browser can load it directly from your
      site. This guide does not upload files or change the project's styles.
    </p>
    <ol class="grid list-decimal gap-4 ps-6">
      <li>
        Put your licensed <code>.woff2</code> file in <code>public/fonts/</code>
        . Create the folder if needed.
      </li>
      <li>
        Add an <code>@font-face</code> rule to{" "}
        <code>src/styles/themes/fonts.css</code>, below the existing imports.
        Use your actual font name, file name, weight, and style.
        <pre class="my-3 overflow-x-auto rounded-lg border border-canvas-page-border p-4 text-sm"><code>{fontExample}</code></pre>
        Add a separate rule for each additional weight or style, or a supported
        weight range for a variable font.
      </li>
      <li>
        Inside the existing <code>@theme static</code> block, add a token such
        as <code>--font-example-sans: "Example Sans", sans-serif;</code>. You
        can then use <code>font-example-sans</code> in your markup.
      </li>
      <li>
        To list the font in Typography Builder, add{" "}
        <code>"var(--font-example-sans)": "Example Sans"</code> to{" "}
        <code>typographyFonts</code> in{" "}
        <code>src/data/typography-builder.js</code>. Reopen Typography Builder
        and select it under <strong>Font family</strong>.
      </li>
    </ol>
  </article>
</Layout>
